    id: 'll37-one-blood-truth-over-the-debate',
    stories: [{"kind":"parable","tone":"light","title":"The Wells of Two Banks","body":"The town of Halford sat on two banks of one small river, and its people kept a great and serious custom: everyone knew which bank your grandparents came from. Eastbank folk were said to be honest but stubborn; Westbank folk clever but sly. Mothers warned their children. Merchants checked your family name before they smiled. The two sides drew their water from two different wells and were quietly certain their own well was the purer. One dry August the river shrank to a trickle and both famous wells ran low. The council, in a panic, hired an old surveyor to find out why. He walked the banks, dropped his lines, studied his charts, and called the whole town together at the bridge. \"I have wonderful news and awkward news,\" he said. \"Your two wells are fed by the same underground spring. It runs beneath the riverbed. Eastbank and Westbank have been drinking, all these years, the very same water.\" There was a long, uncomfortable silence. An Eastbank baker looked at a Westbank butcher he had refused to greet for twenty years. The butcher looked back. Somewhere a child giggled, and then the whole bridge was laughing at itself, at the wells, the warnings, the family names, the enormous foolishness of it all. They dug a single new well that autumn, at the middle of the bridge, and carved over it a plain sentence: one spring, one water, one town. It was, an old woman said, the truest thing anyone in Halford had ever written down. For all their sorting and suspicion, they had always been of one source, made by one Maker, drawn from one hidden spring.","verse":"Acts 17:26"},{"kind":"parable","tone":"solemn","title":"The Sentence He Never Earned","body":"Everyone in the market knew the boy's father had been a thief. The man was long gone, jailed, then dead, but the town kept his sentence alive in the son. Shopkeepers watched Eli's hands. Mothers pulled their children a step closer when he passed. When bread went missing from a stall, three fingers pointed at him before anyone thought to look. He had stolen nothing in his life, but he wore his father's crime like a coat he had never been allowed to take off. One winter a purse vanished at the grain merchant's, and the cry went up at once for Eli. They dragged him before the old magistrate, a gray man who had judged the town for forty years. The crowd expected the usual quick verdict. The magistrate did not look at the boy's face; he looked at the crowd. \"Whose purse was taken?\" he asked. \"The merchant's.\" \"And whose hand took it?\" No one could say. \"Then why,\" he said, rising, \"do I keep hearing his father's name in this room? The father answered for his own deeds, and answers still. This boy has his own account, and I find nothing written against him.\" He turned to Eli. \"The debt was never yours to carry. Set it down.\" The market went quiet. An old woman who had crossed the street from Eli for years came, that evening, and crossed back, and brought him bread. The town had confused a bloodline with a soul, and the confusion had nearly crushed an innocent boy. But a son does not bear his father's iniquity. Every soul stands, and falls, and is answered for, alone.","verse":"Ezekiel 18:20"}],
